  "success": true,
  "output": "I have a complete picture of the codebase. Here's the full performance analysis:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis: convert_pro\n\nThis is a Rails 8 + Stimulus app. No React means no re-render concerns — I'll cover the JS equivalent (unnecessary DOM work) under memory instead.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 / Repeated Query Patterns\n\n**A. `ToolRegistry.tools.find` called per row in views** (high frequency)\n\n`conversions/index.html.erb:36` and `pages/dashboard.html.erb:131` both do:\n```erb\n<% tool_meta = ToolRegistry.tools.find { |t| t[:class_name] == conversion.tool_name } %>\n```\n\nThis scans 26 tools for every row rendered. Fix: build a hash lookup once before the loop.\n\n```ruby\n# In ToolRegistry\nTOOL_BY_CLASS_NAME = tools.index_by { |t| t[:class_name] }.freeze\n\ndef self.find_by_class_name(class_name)\n  TOOL_BY_CLASS_NAME[class_name]\nend\n```\n\nThen in the view:\n```erb\n<% tool_lookup = ToolRegistry::TOOL_BY_CLASS_NAME %>\n<% @conversions.each do |conversion| %>\n  <% tool_meta = tool_lookup[conversion.tool_name] %>\n```\n\n**B. `output_file.attached?` queried per row** (`pages/dashboard.html.erb:151`)\n\nInside `@recent_conversions.each`, `conversion.output_file.attached?` fires a separate query per row because Active Storage doesn't eager-load by default. Fix the controller:\n\n```ruby\n# pages_controller.rb\n@recent_conversions = current_user.conversions\n  .includes(:output_file_attachment)\n  .order(created_at: :desc).limit(5)\n```\n\n**C. `conversions/index` has no pagination**\n\n```ruby\n# conversions_controller.rb:5\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc)\n```\n\nThis loads the user's entire history. A user with 500 conversions loads 500 rows. Add a limit or use kaminari (already installed):\n\n```ruby\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc).page(params[:page]).per(20)\n```\n\n**D. Admin dashboard fires 13 separate COUNT queries**\n\n`admin/dashboard_controller.rb:7–44` makes 13 individual `User.where(...).count` calls. Combine the user stats into two queries:\n\n```ruby\n# Single query for user breakdowns\nuser_stats = User.group(:plan, :provider).select(\n  \"plan, provider, COUNT(*) as count, COUNT(confirmed_at) as confirmed_count\"\n).load\n\n# Or use a single SQL with conditional aggregation\nUser.pick(\n  \"COUNT(*)\", \n  \"COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE plan = 1)\",           # pro\n  \"COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE confirmed_at IS NOT NULL)\",\n  \"COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE provider = 'google_oauth2')\"\n)\n# => [@total_users, @pro_users, @confirmed_users, @google_users]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Missing Database Indexes\n\nThe schema has **no index on `conversions.created_at`**, `conversions.status`, `users.plan`, or `users.confirmed_at` — all of which are used in time-range scans across the whole table.\n\n```ruby\n# New migration\nadd_index :conversions, :created_at\nadd_index :conversions, :status\nadd_index :users, :plan\nadd_index :users, :confirmed_at\n```\n\nThe dashboard, cleanup job, and analytics controller all do `WHERE created_at > ?` or `WHERE status = ?` full-table scans without these.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching Opportunities\n\n**A. ToolRegistry is already in memory but `find` is O(N) per call**\n\nThe fix from issue 1A (hash index) handles this. The existing `cattr_accessor` approach is fine; just replace linear `find` with the hash.\n\n**B. `ToolRegistry.categories` recomputes on every call**\n\n```ruby\n# tool_registry.rb:293\ndef self.categories\n  tools.group_by { |t| t[:category] }\nend\n```\n\nThis re-runs on every request. Since `tools` is a constant array, memoize it:\n\n```ruby\nCATEGORIES = tools.group_by { |t| t[:category] }.freeze\n\ndef self.categories = CATEGORIES\n```\n\n**C. Admin dashboard counts could be cached short-term**\n\nThese stats don't need to be real-time for an admin panel. Wrap with Solid Cache (already configured):\n\n```ruby\n# admin/dashboard_controller.rb\n@total_users = Rails.cache.fetch(\"admin/total_users\", expires_in: 5.minutes) { User.count }\n```\n\n**D. `AnalyticsController` fires 3 queries that can be 1**\n\n```ruby\n# analytics_controller.rb — current: 3 COUNT queries\n@total_conversions = current_user.conversions.count\n@recent_conversions = current_user.conversions.where('created_at > ?', 30.days.ago).count\n@older_conversions  = current_user.conversions.where('created_at BETWEEN ? AND ?', 60.days.ago, 30.days.ago).count\n\n# Replace with one query\ncounts = current_user.conversions.pick(\n  \"COUNT(*)\",\n  \"COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE created_at > ?)\",\n  \"COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE created_at BETWEEN ? AND ?)\",\n  30.days.ago, 60.days.ago, 30.days.ago\n)\n@total_conversions, @recent_conversions, @older_conversions = counts\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory Concerns (JavaScript)\n\n**A. `processSplitPdf` and `processPdfToImages` hold all pages in RAM simultaneously** (`client_tool_controller.js:98–204`)\n\nBoth methods build the full `zipData` object before zipping — for a 100-page PDF at 2× scale, this can be 200–500 MB in canvas buffers. Process pages in batches and stream to the zip:\n\n```js\n// processPdfToImages — process pages lazily instead of pre-loading all\n// Use fflate's Zip stream API instead of zipSync for large files:\nimport { Zip, ZipPassThrough } from \"fflate\"\n\n// accumulate a stream instead of building the full zipData object first\n```\n\nThis is the highest real-world risk in the codebase for a user who uploads a large PDF.\n\n**B. `downloadBlob` leaks the object URL on errors** (`client_tool_controller.js:269`)\n\n```js\ndownloadBlob(blob, filename) {\n    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob)\n    const a = document.createElement('a')\n    a.href = url\n    a.download = filename\n    document.body.appendChild(a)\n    a.click()\n    setTimeout(() => {\n        document.body.removeChild(a)\n        URL.revokeObjectURL(url)  // ← not called if an exception throws above\n    }, 0)\n}\n```\n\nIf `a.click()` throws, the object URL is never revoked. Wrap in try/finally:\n\n```js\ndownloadBlob(blob, filename) {\n    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob)\n    try {\n        const a = Object.assign(document.createElement('a'), { href: url, download: filename })\n        document.body.appendChild(a)\n        a.click()\n        document.body.removeChild(a)\n    } finally {\n        URL.revokeObjectURL(url)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant Computations\n\n**`ConversionLimiter` re-evaluates conditions on every call** but reads from a counter cache column (`conversions_count`) so it's cheap. No change needed here — the counter cache is the right call.\n\n**`ConversionsController#show:39`** calls `ToolRegistry.tools.find { |t| t[:class_name] == @conversion.tool_name }` — once per request, not a loop. The hash index from issue 1A fixes this too.\n\n---\n\n### Priority Order\n\n| Priority | Issue | Impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| 🔴 High | Missing index on `conversions.created_at` | Every time-range query is a full scan |\n| 🔴 High | `output_file.attached?` N+1 in dashboard loop | Extra query per conversion shown |\n| 🟡 Medium | Unbounded `conversions/index` query | OOM risk for heavy users |\n| 🟡 Medium | `ToolRegistry` linear scan per row | CPU waste per page render |\n| 🟡 Medium | `processPdfToImages` / `processSplitPdf` all-in-memory | OOM for large PDFs in browser |\n| 🟢 Low | Admin dashboard 13 COUNT queries | Admin-only, acceptable |\n| 🟢 Low | `ToolRegistry.categories` recomputed per call | Trivial, easy one-liner fix |\n",
